  "Number": 11,
  "Question": "If the perimeter of rectangle \\(ABCD\\) is \\(20\\) inches, the least value of diagonal \\(AC,\\) in inches, is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(0\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\sqrt{50}\\)",
    "C": "\\(10\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\sqrt{200}\\)",
    "E": "none of these"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "The perimeter fixes the sum of two adjacent sides. Let \\(AB=u\\) and \\(BC=v\\), measured in inches. Since both side lengths are positive,\n\\[\n2(u+v)=20,\n\\qquad v=10-u,\n\\qquad 0<u<10.\n\\]\nThe diagonal is the hypotenuse of right triangle \\(ABC\\), so the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nAC^2&=u^2+(10-u)^2\\\\\n&=2u^2-20u+100\\\\\n&=2(u-5)^2+50.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nWriting the expression as a square plus a constant reveals its minimum. Because \\((u-5)^2\\ge0\\), we have \\(AC^2\\ge50\\). Equality holds when \\(u=5\\), which gives \\(v=5\\). This square has perimeter \\(20\\), so the bound is attained. Taking the positive square root, the least diagonal is \\(\\boxed{\\sqrt{50}}\\) inches, choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).",
